Certainly! Below is a simple JavaScript function that takes a `canvas` element as a parameter and draws a square on it. The function assumes that the square will be drawn in the center of the canvas and will have a side length of 100 pixels. You can adjust the size and position of the square as needed.

```javascript
function drawSquare(canvas) {
    // Get the 2D rendering context
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

    // Set the canvas width and height to match the element's dimensions
    canvas.width = canvas.clientWidth;
    canvas.height = canvas.clientHeight;

    // Define the position and size of the square
    const squareSize = 100;
    const x = (canvas.width - squareSize) / 2;
    const y = (canvas.height - squareSize) / 2;

    // Set the fill color (optional)
    ctx.fillStyle = 'blue';

    // Draw the square
    ctx.fillRect(x, y, squareSize, squareSize);
}

// Example usage:
// Assuming you have a canvas element with the id "myCanvas"
const canvas = document.getElementById('myCanvas');
drawSquare(canvas);
```

### Explanation:
- **`canvas.getContext('2d')`**: This gets the 2D rendering context for the canvas, which is used to draw shapes, images, etc.
- **`canvas.width` and `canvas.height`**: These are set to match the client width and height of the canvas element to ensure the drawing is responsive.
- **`squareSize`**: This variable defines the size of the square. You can change this value to make the square larger or smaller.
- **`x` and `y`**: These variables calculate the position of the square so that it is centered on the canvas.
- **`ctx.fillStyle`**: This sets the color of the square. You can change the color by modifying this line.
- **`ctx.fillRect(x, y, squareSize, squareSize)`**: This draws the square on the canvas.

Make sure to include this script in your HTML file and ensure that the canvas element has an ID that matches the one used in the example (`myCanvas`).
